update public.curriculum_units as c set description = v.description from (values
('0277b1fb-dea4-4a82-bf9c-6093367f75d1'::uuid, 'The absence of specialised excretory organs does not mean plants lack waste products. This theme examines the ways plants deal with substances formed during metabolism, including diffusion, storage and removal when leaves or bark are shed.'),
('09140950-b021-47da-bdf9-1f770d3fe450'::uuid, 'Plants do not have specialised excretory organs like those in humans, yet their cells still produce substances that must be managed. This section examines how plants remove, store or reuse waste products, including the release of gases and the storage of substances in leaves, bark and other tissues.'),
('18ab4bca-e93b-4b2f-b071-2afe7ceb7fe3'::uuid, 'After identifying the wastes that must be removed, turn to the organs responsible for removing them from the blood. This theme introduces the human urinary system and the route taken by urine. It provides the structural background needed to understand how the kidneys maintain the body''s internal conditions.'),
('2a515cc6-6051-4de8-ae8e-efd476513d50'::uuid, 'A plant''s roots absorb water and mineral salts, but these materials are needed throughout the plant. In this lesson, you will explain why transport is necessary, identify xylem and phloem as the main transport tissues, and distinguish the substances each carries. You will build a foundation for studying water movement and the distribution of food.'),
('41a77872-e03a-4d10-add9-5265e736bbd5'::uuid, 'The urinary system carries urine away, but the kidneys first control what remains in the blood. This theme focuses on kidney structure and function, introducing the nephron and the processes through which useful substances are retained while wastes and excess materials are removed.'),
('4ad3cb96-f51d-45ad-b3ed-110604d2b21b'::uuid, 'Metabolism produces waste substances that must be removed from the body. In this lesson, you will define excretion, distinguish it from egestion and identify common excretory products, including carbon dioxide, excess water, salts and nitrogenous wastes. You will explain why their removal is important for normal body function.'),
('4f8c1a2d-b0b9-46c9-aaf3-aa2d348fc79f'::uuid, 'Plants manage metabolic waste in several ways without a specialised excretory system. In this lesson, you will describe how gases and excess water can leave the plant, explain how some waste substances are stored in leaves, bark or other tissues, and consider how shedding these parts helps remove stored wastes.'),
('543add2e-db27-4e47-8e10-ae488b80d1c2'::uuid, 'Each kidney contains many nephrons that filter blood and help regulate its composition. In this lesson, you will describe the main regions of a kidney and nephron, explain ultrafiltration and selective reabsorption, and relate urine formation to the removal of metabolic wastes and control of water and salts.'),
('55dab763-3494-4bc9-af57-f324dccd5628'::uuid, 'The need to supply cells with useful materials and remove their wastes leads to specialised transport in animals. This theme introduces the purpose of circulation, the substances carried around the body and the basic features of a transport system. It prepares you to study blood and the organs that keep it moving.'),
('5733ce44-4edd-4926-8c8c-0b8d7aea064c'::uuid, 'The heart maintains blood flow through the lungs and the rest of the body. In this lesson, you will identify the main chambers and major blood vessels of the heart, trace the path of blood through pulmonary and systemic circulation, and distinguish arteries, veins and capillaries by their structure and function.'),
('60fca41d-5347-4458-8208-925e075e5f69'::uuid, 'Blood can transport substances only if it is kept moving through the body. This section examines the heart and the network of blood vessels that form the circulatory system. You will follow the route of blood through the heart and lungs, compare the main types of blood vessels and relate their structure to their functions.'),
('6c27f562-52ac-4f1f-877a-21d760188ed8'::uuid, 'Plants need to move water and mineral salts from their roots to the leaves, while sugars made in the leaves must reach other parts of the plant. This section introduces the tissues and processes responsible for that movement. You will follow the path of water through the plant and examine how manufactured food is distributed to growing and storage regions.'),
('8ae3a02a-887b-4dc1-8270-254b19c38bc9'::uuid, 'The human excretory system filters wastes from the blood and removes them in urine. In this lesson, you will identify the kidneys, ureters, urinary bladder and urethra, describe the function of each organ and trace the pathway followed by urine as it leaves the body.'),
('958c88eb-b693-4b08-9443-47ab1a9e6968'::uuid, 'The substances removed from an organism depend on the chemical reactions taking place in its cells. This theme introduces carbon dioxide, excess water, mineral salts and nitrogenous wastes, and explains why some waste products require careful removal to avoid harming the body.'),
('a0da505f-589d-43f4-a928-2fa208247581'::uuid, 'Blood is made up of plasma and specialised cellular components, each with a particular function. In this lesson, you will identify the main components of blood and explain how red blood cells transport oxygen, white blood cells defend the body, platelets help blood clot and plasma carries dissolved substances.'),
('af8b7c4e-2465-4b6d-a834-5141e57f642b'::uuid, 'The human body removes metabolic wastes through specialised organs that work together as the excretory system. This section examines the kidneys, ureters, urinary bladder and urethra, and follows the movement of urine from its formation to its removal. You will relate the position and structure of these organs to their roles.'),
('bde17d29-fff9-4a18-8063-218bc83e3814'::uuid, 'The kidneys do more than remove waste: they help regulate the amount of water and dissolved salts in the body. This section examines the structure of the kidney and the processes involved in urine formation. You will explore filtration, selective reabsorption and the importance of maintaining a suitable internal balance.'),
('c74e23fd-8ce5-4bd1-88e4-6669c148b7b6'::uuid, 'The components of blood perform their roles as blood circulates through the body. This theme introduces the heart as the muscular pump and the blood vessels as pathways for circulation. It prepares you to trace the route of blood and understand how the structure of the heart supports continuous flow.'),
('ce172462-fb98-44b5-a878-f92114516547'::uuid, 'Every living cell needs a supply of oxygen and nutrients and a way to have waste products carried away. In this lesson, you will explain why transport is necessary in animals, identify the main substances carried by the blood and describe the general role of the circulatory system in connecting body tissues with exchange surfaces.'),
('d4594b58-2961-4bdc-9653-5b0d4d4ea2e6'::uuid, 'As cells carry out metabolism, they produce substances that the body must remove. This section introduces the main excretory products and explains why different organisms produce different forms of nitrogenous waste. You will distinguish excretion from egestion and consider how waste production relates to metabolism and water availability.'),
('d9715c9a-a8f5-4567-a4dd-f8323c3ec1c5'::uuid, 'Blood is the transport medium that links different organs and tissues in the body. This section examines its liquid and cellular components, including plasma, red blood cells, white blood cells and platelets. You will relate the structure of each component to its role in transport, defence and clotting.'),
('ebd6b6b6-bfb4-483c-9f18-091283baa4cd'::uuid, 'Unlike plants, animals obtain nutrients from food and need to deliver them, together with oxygen, to cells throughout the body. This section introduces transport in animals, comparing the requirements of small organisms with those of larger, more active bodies. You will examine why specialised transport systems are needed and how circulation meets these demands.'),
('fc47e4a9-4547-4847-8984-b2b2a71620cd'::uuid, 'Having established why animals need a transport system, focus on the blood that carries materials around the body. This theme introduces plasma and the formed elements of blood, showing how their different structures enable transport, protection against infection and prevention of blood loss.'),
('fcad3369-ca34-401f-9d4b-baabd3090ac5'::uuid, 'The movement of substances in plants begins with the need to supply every living part with materials. This theme introduces the transport tissues and the main pathways through which water, mineral salts and manufactured food move. It prepares you to distinguish the roles of xylem and phloem.')
) as v(id,description) where c.id=v.id and c.description is distinct from v.description;
